          %p A number of examples of taut functors can be deduced by applying the following observation.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}
              be a functor that preserves weak pullbacks, and assume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              has pullbacks. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is taut.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              In the first place,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              preserves monos. For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: A \to B
              is monic if and only if
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi i
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A &amp; \stackrel{1_A}{\to} &amp; A \\
                    \\mathllap{1_A} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{i} \\
                    A &amp; \underset{i}{\to} &amp; B
                    }
            %p
              is a pullback. By hypothesis, the canonical map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: T(A) \to T(A) \times_{T(B)} T(A)
              is a split epimorphism (see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+limit"} here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A) \times_{T(B)} T(A) \to T(A)
              is the identity. Therefore
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is an isomorphism, i.e., applying
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              to the displayed pullback is a pullback, and this forces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(i)
              to be monic.
            %p Now if
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    P &amp; \stackrel{j}{\to} &amp; B \\
                    \\mathllap{q} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{p} \\
                    A &amp; \underset{i}{\to} &amp; C
                    }
            %p
              is a pullback with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              monic, we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              and therefore
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(j)
              is monic. The canonical map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: T(P) \to T(A) \times_{T(C)} T(B)
              is split epic, but also monic, since the mono
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(j)
              factors through it. Thus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is an isomorphism, which completes the proof.
          %p A similar proof shows that weakly cartesian natural transformations are also taut.
          %p As a result of this proposition,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+functor"} cartesian functor
                is (trivially) taut.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter endofunctor
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                is taut. (See
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/relational+beta-module#functor"} here
                for a proof that the ultrafilter functor preserves weak pullbacks.) In fact, the ultrafilter monad is taut.
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter monad
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                is taut.
            %li
              %p
                The covariant power set monad, whose algebras are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sup-lattices"} sup-lattices
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/operad#analytic"} analytic endofunctor
                induced by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} species
                is taut. Furthermore, a morphism of species induces a weakly cartesian transformation between the corresponding analytic functors, thus
                %em a fortiori
                a taut transformation. In particular, an analytic monad is taut.
          %p As an exception, we have
          %ul
            %li
              The double (contravariant) power set functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mi op
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \circ P^{op}: Set \to Set
              is not taut.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Paul Taylor has made tautness of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                a central assumption in his account of induction via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+coalgebras"} well-founded coalgebras
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Taylor"} book
            %li
              %p
                Tautness assumptions play a role in viewing relational
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#CS"} Cruttwell-Shulman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relational+beta-modules"} relational beta-modules
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual double category
                of relations. A taut monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                (such as the ultrafilter monad) induces a monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{T}
                on this virtual double category (that is, a monad in an appropriate 2-category of virtual double categories). From there, one can define a horizontal Kleisli construction which is another virtual double category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi HKl
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Rel
                        %mo ,
                        %mover
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HKl(Rel, \bar{T})
                = succeed "-multicategory" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{T}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Rel
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
                is by definition a monoid in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi HKl
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Rel
                        %mo ,
                        %mover
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HKl(Rel, \bar{T})
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo =
                        %mi β
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = \beta
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
                = succeed "\u201D." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                = succeed "-matrices\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is a completely distributive quantale (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Rel
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
                being the case
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo =
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \mathbf{2}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                a taut monad, one can define a virtual double category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi HKl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mtext -
                      %mi Mat
                      %mo ,
                      %mover
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HKl(V\text{-}Mat, \bar{T})
                and then define generalized multicategories as before. (These were studied in a series of articles by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#CHT2"} Clementino, Hofmann, Tholen
                %a{:href => "#Seal"} Seal
                and others under the name “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo ,
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T, V)
                \-algebras”.)
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Taylor
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Practical Foundations of Mathematics
          %ul#CS
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geoff+Cruttwell"} Geoff Cruttwell
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              = succeed "," do
                %em A unified framework for generalized multicategories
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0907.2460"} arxiv/0907.2460
          %ul#CHT2
            %li
              Maria Manuel Clementino, Dirk Hofmann, and Walter Tholen,
              %em One Setting for All: Metric, Topology, Uniformity, Approach Structure.
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.yorku.ca/~tholen/ProMat_V_.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %ul#Seal
            %li
              Gavin J. Seal,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Canonical and op-canonical lax algebras
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/14/10/14-10abs.html"} web
